{
  "title": "How Compound Interest Works",
  "screens": [
    {
      "title": "A little extra grows",
      "text": "Suppose you leave $100 in a savings account that pays interest. Interest is money the account adds for letting it use your money—a little extra on top of your balance.",
      "imagePrompt": null
    },
    {
      "title": "Interest joins the balance",
      "text": "With compound interest, earned interest stays in the account. The next time interest is added, it can be calculated on both your original money and the interest already earned. That’s interest earning interest.",
      "imagePrompt": "Show a savings account balance as a starting stack of money with a small interest amount added beside it, then show both amounts together forming the base for the next interest addition."
    },
    {
      "title": "Watch $100 grow",
      "text": "At 10% annual interest, $100 earns $10 in the first year, becoming $110. In the second year, 10% of $110 is $11, so the balance becomes $121. This assumes no deposits or withdrawals.",
      "imagePrompt": "Show a two-year timeline: $100 grows to $110 after $10 interest in year one, then to $121 after $11 interest in year two."
    },
    {
      "title": "Debt can compound too",
      "text": "If interest is added to an unpaid balance, later interest may also build on that added amount. A credit-card balance can grow this way when you carry it, depending on the card’s terms.",
      "imagePrompt": "Show an unpaid credit-card balance with an interest amount added to it, then show the larger combined balance as the base for another interest charge."
    },
    {
      "title": "Time and terms matter",
      "text": "With a positive rate, more time usually gives compounding more chances to build. The result also depends on the rate, how often interest is added, and account terms. Check for fees and taxes; a financial professional can review your situation.",
      "imagePrompt": null
    }
